[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/BOe48Lw4"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/pp2b2p/2n1bpp1/3p1p2/1PP5/P3PN2/3NBPPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 1 14"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2q1rk1/pp2b2p/2n1bpp1/3p1p2/1PP5/P3PN2/3NBPPP/R2Q1RK1_w_-_-_1_14"]

{ Welcome to pawn strategy. Our first plan is the pawn break: a pawn push that smashes a locked structure open to free your other pieces. Push to c5. [%cal Yc4c5] }
14. c5 { Pawn to c5, the break! His bishop on e7 has nowhere left to go but d6, and next you roll b5 and c6 to break up his queenside pawns for good. [%csl Gc5][%cal Gb4b5,Gc5c6] } (14. Qb3 { Queen to b3 also presses on the pawn on d5, but he simply trades it off with dxc4 next, and the tension you wanted to keep resolves for free. } 14... dxc4 15. Bxc4 Bxc4 16. Qxc4+ { Check! The central tension is gone, and your bind on the long diagonal goes with it — still better for you, just not by as much as pushing c5 would have earned. }) (14. Qa4 { Queen to a4 lines up on the a column, but he pushes to a5 anyway, and capturing there only trades down to equal — push through with b5 instead and keep the bind alive. } 14... a5 15. b5 Ne5 { Playable, and still clearly better for you — but his knight gets to sit on e5, so you keep less of an edge than the direct c5 push would have given. }) (14. Qc2 { Queen to c2 is quiet, but it lets him grab the a5 square himself, so you spend a move without gaining any ground. } 14... a5 15. b5 Ne5 { Only a little better for you: he got to play a5 himself, claiming the very square your plan wanted to control first. }) (14. Rc1 { Rook to c1 backs up the c pawn, but he simply trades it off with dxc4, and the file opens before your other pieces are ready for it. } 14... dxc4 15. Qa4 a6 16. Bxc4 { The centre pawns are gone and the position is open — still better for you, just without the space c5 would have locked in. }) (14. cxd5 { Taking on d5 releases the tension the wrong way: it hands him the bishop pair's best diagonal instead of squeezing his queenside. } 14... Bxd5 15. Qa4 a6 { His bishop now sits on d5 like a rock in the centre, aiming at your whole position — only a little better for you than if you had kept the tension. [%csl Rd5] }) 14... a6 { He plays a6 to stop b5 for now. Bring the knight to d4 next, where it will support both c5 and b5. [%cal Yf3d4] } 15. Nd4 { Knight to d4: it attacks his knight on c6, and once the trade lands on d4, that pawn will support both c5 and b5. [%csl Gd4] } (15. Nb3 { Knight to b3 also works: from there it defends both the c5 and a5 squares, backing up the same plan a different way. } 15... Bf7 16. Nfd4 Ne5 { Still clearly winning: the knight reaches d4 anyway, just one move slower than going there directly. }) (15. Re1 { Rook to e1 develops, but the knight to d4 was already the move — this just delays the same plan by a tempo. } 15... Qd7 16. Nd4 Nxd4 17. exd4 { The knight still lands on d4 all the same — you only spent an extra move getting the rook to e1 first. }) (15. Qc2 { Queen to c2 also works: it tucks the queen in safely, and the knight still heads for d4 right after. } 15... Re8 16. Nd4 Nxd4 17. exd4 { The same knight lands on d4 either way — this time with your queen sitting on c2 instead of behind the rook on e1. }) (15. Rb1 { Rook to b1 is slow: it does nothing for the knight's trip to d4, and he uses the free move to tidy his position. } 15... Qd7 16. Re1 Rfe8 { Still clearly better for you, but the rook move gained nothing for the plan — you still have to regroup and send the knight to d4. }) 15... Nxd4 { He trades knights. Take back with the pawn, and it lands ready to support the whole queenside plan. [%cal Ye3d4] } 16. exd4 { The pawn takes back. Now the pawn on c5 has a friend on d4, and b5 followed by c6 is on its way to wreck his queenside pawns for good. [%csl Gd4,Gc5][%cal Gb4b5,Gc5c6][%tail] } 16... Re8 17. b5 17... axb5 18. Bxb5 { The bishop recaptures on b5. His last queenside pawn stands alone on b7, with no a or c pawn ever able to defend it again. [%csl Rb7] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, right away"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/Bzn7Sw7H"]
[FEN "r4rk1/2R3bp/5ppn/p2pp3/4b3/1P2P3/PB1NBPPP/3R2K1 b - - 3 19"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/2R3bp/5ppn/p2pp3/4b3/1P2P3/PB1NBPPP/3R2K1_b_-_-_3_19"]

{ You have been aiming at the pawn break e4 all game, and he keeps hitting your pieces to stall it. When do you finally push it through? }
19... Bf5 { His bishop lands back on f5, staring straight at your e4 square. Do you spend a move chasing it away, or push the break right now? } 20. e4 { Right away! Never mind the pawn — the hit on his bishop on f5 matters far more, and pushing now keeps the initiative on your side. [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge4f5] } (20. Bb5 { Bishop to b5 finds a nice square, but it stalls: the pawn break to e4 is still sitting there waiting to be played. } 20... Rfc8 21. Rdc1 Rxc7 22. Rxc7 { Still clearly better for you, but the break never went in — the bishop trip cost a move you did not need to spend. }) (20. a4 { Pawn to a4, out on the wing, does nothing for the pawn break you actually came here to play. } 20... Rfd8 21. h3 Be6 { He gets a free move to tidy his position, so you end up only a little better than if you had pushed the break at once. }) (20. Bf3 { Bishop to f3 attacks the pawn on d5, but he simply pushes past to e4, and his central pawns start rolling forward together. } 20... Be6 21. Nb1 e4 { His pawns roll forward together, e4 among them — only a little better for you than pushing the break yourself would have been. [%csl Re4] }) (20. h3 { Pawn to h3 chases the bishop on f5 away first, but that gives him the time to push his own pawn to d4. } 20... Rfd8 21. g4 Be6 22. e4 d4 { He gets a passed pawn on d4 with another pawn right behind to defend it — level chances now, all because you paused to chase the bishop. [%csl Rd4] }) 20... dxe4 { He recaptures with the d pawn. His king is one move from a check from your dark bishop — find it. } 21. Bc4+ { Check! The bishop swoops to c4 along the very diagonal his own capture just opened. [%cal Gc4g8] } (21. h3 { Pawn to h3 spends a move chasing the bishop, while the real business — the check on c4 — is still waiting to be played. } 21... Rfd8 22. Bc3 a4 { Still clearly better for you, but he gets the move to untangle his pieces while you were busy on the rim. }) (21. Nc4 { Knight to c4 heads straight for the corner, hunting the rook on a8. } 21... Rf7 22. Nb6 Rxc7 23. Nxa8 { It is an even trade of rooks, not a material win — but the knight escapes back into play through b6, and his weak pawns on a5 and e4 still leave you clearly better. [%csl Ra8] }) (21. Nxe4 { Knight takes back the pawn on e4, but he simply recaptures with the bishop, and now the bishop pair is his instead of yours. } 21... Bxe4 22. Rdd7 Nf5 { Equal: the bishop pair you gave up here is worth exactly what the pawn would have been. [%csl Re4] }) (21. Bc3 { Bishop to c3 attacks the pawn on a5. } 21... a4 22. bxa4 Rfc8 { Only a little better for you: the pawn on a5 falls, but he gets his rook to c8 before you can press the advantage further. }) 21... Kh8 { He tucks his king into the corner on h8, safe from the check. Now bring the knight into the attack. } 22. Nf1 { Knight to f1, heading for e3: from there it attacks the pawn on e4 and eyes f5 as well. [%cal Gf1e3,Ge3f5][%csl Re4] } (22. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it does nothing for the knight's trip to e3. } 22... Rfd8 23. Bc3 Bf8 { Only a little better for you — a useful move on its own, but he spends the free tempo tidying his position instead of yours. }) (22. Bc3 { Bishop to c3 attacks the pawn on a5 too, but he simply trades a pair of rooks and eases the pressure. } 22... Rfd8 23. Re1 Rd7 { Only a little better: the bishop still hits a5 either way, but the rook trade costs you some of your pressure on his position. }) (22. Re1 { Rook to e1 also attacks the pawn on e4, but he simply trades off a pair of rooks and simplifies toward the position he wants. } 22... Rfc8 23. Rxc8+ Rxc8 { Only a little better for you: the position simplifies, and trading pieces down is exactly the plan he wanted. }) (22. a4 { Pawn to a4 is a quiet move out on the wing, far from where the real fight over e4 is happening. } 22... Rfd8 23. Bc3 Ng4 { Only a little better for you: the pawn move is harmless enough, but his knight jumps into g4 while your attention was on the wing. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, open the file"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/BSRC5LSZ"]
[FEN "r6r/4kp1p/p2b1p2/2pp4/4p3/1P2P2P/P2PNPP1/2R2RK1 w - - 2 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r6r/4kp1p/p2b1p2/2pp4/4p3/1P2P2P/P2PNPP1/2R2RK1_w_-_-_2_17"]

{ His doubled pawns on the f column are a weakness in name only, until a pawn break opens a file onto them. Which pawn moves? [%csl Rf6,Rf7] }
17. f3 { Pawn to f3 attacks his pawn on e4 head on, and once the trade happens the f column opens straight onto his doubled pawns. [%cal Gf3e4,Gf1f7][%csl Rf7][%tail] } (17. f4 { Pawn to f4 closes the position instead of opening it, and his doubled pawns sit safe behind the wall for good. } 17... f5 18. Rc3 Rhc8 { Only a little better for you: the position stays safe and simple, but his doubled pawns are now out of reach for good. [%csl Rf4] }) (17. Rfd1 { Rook to d1 develops, but the whole point here was opening the f column onto his weak pawns — this does nothing for that. } 17... Rac8 18. d3 f5 { Still clearly better for you, but the f column never opened, so his doubled pawns just sit there untouched. }) (17. Rc3 { Rook to c3 lifts toward the kingside, but he simply trades a pair of pawns and eases his defence. } 17... a5 18. Rfc1 Rhb8 { Only a little better for you: the rook lift is natural enough, but letting him trade first eases his defence of the doubled pawns. }) (17. g3 { Pawn to g3 gives your king some air, but it spends a move while his doubled pawns sit completely untouched. } 17... f5 18. Rfd1 Rac8 { Only a little better for you: the luft is nice to have, but his doubled pawns get a free move to start sorting themselves out. }) 17... f5 18. fxe4 18... fxe4 19. Rf5 { The rook lands on f5, deep in his camp: the pawns on f7 and d5 are both in range along the rank and file. [%csl Rf7,Rd5] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, do not prepare it"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/wriejx5M"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/pb1nbppp/1p1ppn2/2p5/2PP4/1PN1PN2/PB2BPPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 3 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2q1rk1/pb1nbppp/1p1ppn2/2p5/2PP4/1PN1PN2/PB2BPPP/R2Q1RK1_w_-_-_3_10"]

{ You have a central pawn break sitting on d5, ready to play. Do you prepare it first, or push it right now? }
10. d5 { Play the pawn break right now! Pushing to d5 cramps his whole position, and e4 comes next — wait to prepare it, and he gets time to stop it. [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge3e4][%tail] } (10. Qc2 { Queen to c2 prepares the break you could simply play right now. } 10... cxd4 11. Nxd4 Rc8 { He trades on d4 first with cxd4, and the central tension you wanted to keep is gone. Equal now. }) (10. Rc1 { Rook to c1 develops soundly, but he simply trades on d4 first, taking some of the sting out of the break. } 10... a6 11. Bd3 cxd4 { Only a little better for you: a sound developing move, but letting him trade on d4 first takes some sting out of the break. }) (10. Re1 { Rook to e1 is slow: the break is ready to play right now, and this move does not play it. } 10... a6 11. Qc2 cxd4 { Only a little better for you: the break still comes eventually, just a move after it was actually ready to play. }) (10. dxc5 { Taking on c5 releases the central tension for nothing: his knight simply recaptures onto a strong post. } 10... Nxc5 11. Qc2 Rc8 { His knight lands on the strong c5 square, and the game levels out completely. [%csl Gc5] }) (10. h3 { Pawn to h3 shuffles on the rim, far from where this game will actually be decided. } 10... a6 11. Rc1 Re8 { Only a little better for you: a harmless move on its own, but it hands him a free tempo to improve his rook. }) 10... h6 11. e4 { And there it is: pawn to e4. Three pawns stand abreast on c4, d5 and e4, and every black piece is boxed in behind that wall. [%csl Gc4,Gd5,Ge4] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/Ofcz2Nc4"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/4bppp/p1n1pn2/1p6/3P4/P1N2N2/BP3PPP/R1BQR1K1 w - - 0 13"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/4bppp/p1n1pn2/1p6/3P4/P1N2N2/BP3PPP/R1BQR1K1_w_-_-_0_13"]

{ New plan: the isolani, an isolated pawn with no friend on either neighbouring column. Weak in the endgame, strong right now. His knight left d5. Push! [%cal Yd4d5] }
13. d5 { The break! With his knight gone from d5, the pawn advances there attacking the knight on c6 and the pawn on e6, and every black piece is still stuck at home to meet it. [%csl Gd5] } (13. Qd3 { Queen to d3 prepares the same break, but he gets there first with his own break, b4. } 13... Bb7 14. Bg5 b4 { His break on b4 lands before yours does, and the d5 break you wanted is gone. Only a little better for you. }) (13. Bg5 { Bishop to g5 develops naturally, but the moment for the d5 break passes while you do. } 13... h6 14. Bh4 b4 { Only a little better for you: the bishop gets developed, but his own break on b4 still gets in regardless. }) (13. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it gives him time to get set for the d5 break before you even play it. } 13... Bb7 14. d5 Nxd5 { He is ready for the break now and recaptures cleanly on d5. Only a little better for you than if you had pushed it at once. }) (13. Ne5 { Knight to e5 jumps into the centre, but it leaves the pawn on d4 undefended, and he simply takes it. } 13... Nxd4 14. Be3 Nf5 { Worse: a full pawn down, and the knight only shuffles back to f5 to show for it. [%csl Rd4] }) 13... exd5 { He captures on d5 with the pawn. Take back with the knight, landing right in the heart of his position. [%cal Yc3d5] } 14. Nxd5 { The knight lands on d5, forking the bishop on e7 and the knight on f6 at once — a monster in the middle of his position. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd5e7,Gd5f6] } (14. Bxd5 { Bishop takes back on d5 instead, but it invites a wave of trades that drains all the pressure away. } 14... Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Be6 { Once the trades finish, the pressure on his position is gone completely. Equal now. }) (14. Qd3 { Queen to d3 is slow: it gives him a free move to organise his defence around the knight on d5. } 14... Be6 15. Ng5 Qd7 { He catches up in development while your queen found a square instead of pressing. Slightly worse for you. }) (14. Bg5 { Bishop to g5 pins the knight on f6, but he simply untangles and the pin comes to nothing. } 14... Be6 15. Rc1 Rc8 { Slightly worse for you: the pin never really bites, and he wriggles the knight free without trouble. }) 14... Ra7 { He tucks his rook onto a7, ready to defend the bishop on e7 if the knight forks there. Now develop with a hit. [%cal Yc1e3] } 15. Be3 { Bishop to e3 joins the attack, aiming straight down the diagonal at his rook on a7 and the pawn on b6. [%cal Ge3a7][%csl Gd5] } (15. Bf4 { Bishop to f4 also works: a different diagonal, but the same idea of piling every piece onto the attack. } 15... Rd7 16. Nxe7+ Nxe7 { Still clearly better for you: trading knights on e7 removes the one piece he had doing anything active. }) (15. Nxe7+ { Knight takes the bishop on e7 with check, trading away the monster on d5 a move early. } 15... Rxe7 16. Bd2 Bg4 { Slightly better only: trading the strong knight away early gives back some of the advantage you had built up. }) (15. Qc2 { Queen to c2 develops, but he simply takes the knight on d5 and the central bind disappears. } 15... Nxd5 16. Qxc6 Bb7 { Equal: the queen move wins nothing back for the central pawn he just grabbed on d5. }) (15. h3 { Pawn to h3 gives your king air, but it does nothing about the fork already coming on e7. } 15... Be6 16. Nxe7+ Rxe7 { Only a little better for you: the fork on e7 still lands, but luft first means you gain less from it than the direct plan would have. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, keep the lid on"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/Tn2qTkzD"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/pp2bppp/2n1p3/3n4/2BP4/2N2N2/PP3PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 1 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/pp2bppp/2n1p3/3n4/2BP4/2N2N2/PP3PPP/R1BQ1RK1_w_-_-_1_10"]

{ His pawn on d4 is the isolani, isolated, and your knight on d5 blocks it cold. Prying it loose is his whole plan. Keep the lid on. }
10. Re1 { His rook lines up on the e column, adding pressure down the centre. Which of your pieces must never leave d5? [%csl Gd5] } 10... Bf6 { Bishop to f6 piles more pressure onto the pawn on d4, while the knight on d5 stays put — his pawn can never push forward now. [%cal Gf6d4][%csl Gd5][%tail] } (10... Nf6 { Knight to f6 abandons the blockade, and his break on d5 is back on the table. } 11. a3 b6 12. Ba2 Bb7 13. Bf4 { Slightly worse for you: with the knight gone from d5, his pawn is one push away from breaking through. [%cal Rd4d5] }) (10... a6 { Pawn to a6 is slow: it does nothing to keep your knight anchored on d5, and the blockade slips soon after. } 11. Bb3 Nf6 12. a3 b5 13. d5 { There it is: the pawn breaks through to d5 once the blockading knight wanders off to f6. Slightly worse for you. [%csl Rd5] }) (10... Bd6 { Bishop to d6 finds a nice diagonal, but it does nothing to pressure the isolated pawn on d4 the way the bishop on f6 does. } 11. Bb3 h6 12. Bd2 Nce7 13. Ne5 { Slightly worse for you: White claims a strong knight outpost on e5, while your bishop on d6 never joined the fight against the pawn on d4. }) (10... Nxc3 { Knight takes on c3, trading away the blockader for damaged white pawns — but it also hands White the bishop pair and open lines toward your king. } 11. bxc3 b6 12. h4 Bb7 13. Ng5 { Slightly worse for you: his pieces come storming at your king, and the doubled pawns you won turn out to matter less than the safety you gave up. }) (10... h6 { Pawn to h6 gives your king a little air, but it does nothing to reinforce the blockade on d5. } 11. Qe2 Bd6 12. Bd2 b6 { Slightly worse for you: the luft move did nothing for the blockade, and White uses the free tempo to catch up in development. }) 11. h3 11... Nb6 { The knight shifts to b6, but it still guards d5 from there — and now the knight on c6 and the bishop on f6 both hit the pawn on d4 directly. It is going nowhere. [%csl Rd4] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, chase the bishop"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/FMxJOcuY"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/1p1bbppp/p1n1pn2/6B1/3P4/1BN2N2/PP1Q1PPP/R4RK1 w - - 5 12"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2q1rk1/1p1bbppp/p1n1pn2/6B1/3P4/1BN2N2/PP1Q1PPP/R4RK1_w_-_-_5_12"]

{ He has the isolani on d4, and every piece is in place to push it through to d5. His bishop on b3 is what makes that push work — chase it away. [%csl Rb3][%cal Rd4d5] }
12. Rad1 { His rook joins the attack on d1, and the pawn break to d5 is in the air. Which of his pieces must you chase away first? [%cal Rd4d5] } 12... Na5 { Knight to a5 chases the bishop off the b3 to f7 diagonal, and without it guarding the push, the d5 break loses its bite. [%cal Ga5b3][%csl Rb3] } (12... Rc8 { Rook to c8 develops naturally, but it does nothing to stop the break, and d5 lands next move. } 13. d5 exd5 14. Nxd5 Be6 15. Nxe7+ { Slightly worse for you: the centre opens while your pieces are not ready for it, and the knight fork on e7 trades a knight for your bishop with check. [%csl Rd5] }) (12... Nb4 { Knight to b4 tries to go around the bishop the other way, but it heads nowhere useful. } 13. Ne5 Bc6 14. f4 { Worse for you: his pieces simply roll forward while your knight sits offside on b4. }) (12... h6 { Pawn to h6 kicks the bishop, but it simply takes the pawn back and rips your king side open. } 13. Bxh6 Nd5 14. Rfe1 { Worse for you: a pawn is gone and your king side is wide open. [%csl Rh6] }) (12... Re8 { Rook to e8 is slow: it does nothing to stop the break, and playing h6 later walks into the very same trick. } 13. Rfe1 h6 14. Bxh6 { Worse for you: the very same bishop sacrifice on h6 lands all over again. }) (12... Qa5 { Queen to a5 comes out actively, but it does nothing to stop the coming break. } 13. Rfe1 Rad8 14. h3 { Worse for you: the queen move wastes the very tempo the bishop chase needed. }) 13. Bc2 { The bishop retreats to c2, out of the knight's reach. Keep chasing: there is more to gain here. } 13... Nc4 { Knight to c4 forks the queen and the pawn on b2, so the queen must move and White loses a valuable tempo. [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc4d2] } (13... Rc8 { Rook to c8 also works, but the knight to c4 was already the stronger move sitting right there. } 14. Bd3 Nc4 15. Bxc4 Rxc4 { Equal: the knight still lands on c4 either way, just one move later than planned. }) (13... Bc6 { Bishop to c6 develops, but it costs a tempo, and his knight jumps into the strong e5 outpost. } 14. Ne5 b5 15. Qd3 { Slightly worse for you: his knight claims e5 while your bishop was busy relocating. [%csl Re5] }) (13... g6 { Pawn to g6 weakens the dark squares around your king for no real gain. } 14. Ne5 Bb5 15. Rfe1 { Worse for you: the weakening pawn move does not even stop the knight jumping into e5 anyway. }) (13... Bb5 { Bishop to b5 comes out actively, but it opens the h column for nothing useful. } 14. Rfe1 Nc4 15. Qf4 { Worse for you: the h column opened for nothing, and the knight still lands on c4 regardless. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, take it on purpose"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/KG53RfuA"]
[FEN "r2qkb1r/3npppp/p1p2n2/3p4/NpPP4/1P2PQ1P/P2B1PP1/R3KB1R b KQkq - 1 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qkb1r/3npppp/p1p2n2/3p4/NpPP4/1P2PQ1P/P2B1PP1/R3KB1R_b_KQkq_-_1_10"]

{ Your bishop is the good one here, and his knight sits far from d4. Is taking on the isolani — an isolated pawn — a price worth paying for that bishop's diagonal? [%csl Ra4] }
10... e5 { Yes! Pawn to e5 takes on the isolani on purpose, and in return your bishop gets the whole b8 to h2 diagonal to work with. [%cal Ge5d4][%csl Ra4] } (10... Ne4 { Knight to e4 jumps in, but he simply trades it off, and your position stays solid but passive. } 11. Bd3 Nxd2 12. Kxd2 e6 { Slightly worse for you: solid, but a bit passive — you never got the bishop diagonal the isolani plan offers. }) (10... Qb8 { Queen to b8 tucks out of the way, but it does nothing to contest the centre. } 11. g4 e5 12. g5 { Slightly worse for you: his king side pawns come storming forward while your queen sits passively on b8. }) (10... e6 { Pawn to e6 is solid, but your bishop stays stuck behind its own pawns instead of reaching for the long diagonal. } 11. Bd3 Be7 12. e4 { Slightly worse for you: he keeps the whole centre, and your bishop on c8 has nothing to do. [%csl Rc8] }) (10... h5 { Pawn to h5 is slow, out on the rim, while the centre is where this game will actually be decided. } 11. cxd5 cxd5 12. Rc1 { Worse for you: the rim pawn move ignores the centre entirely, and he claims it for free. }) (10... dxc4 { Taking on c4 opens the position, but his bishop recaptures there with a hit on your queen side. } 11. Bxc4 a5 12. Rc1 { Worse for you: the pawn on c6 hangs to his queen, and his pieces are already lined up to punish it. [%csl Rc6] }) 11. Qe2 { His queen steps back to e2, out of the way. Bring your bishop out to its new diagonal. } 11... Bd6 { Bishop to d6 eyes the pawn on h2 down the long diagonal, and it also guards your pawn on e5. [%cal Gd6h2] } (11... h5 { Pawn to h5 first also works: gaining space on the wing before developing the bishop. } 12. g3 Bd6 { The bishop still comes to d6 next move, just with the h pawn already advanced. }) (11... Be7 { Bishop to e7 chooses the wrong diagonal, and he simply closes the centre in response. } 12. g3 e4 { Slightly worse for you: your bishop sits passively on e7 instead of the active diagonal you paid a pawn for. }) (11... Ne4 { Knight to e4 jumps in first, delaying the bishop's trip to d6 by a move. } 12. g3 Bd6 13. Bg2 { Fine, but slower: the bishop still reaches d6, just one move later than going there directly. }) (11... g6 { Pawn to g6 gives your king some air, but it weakens the square f6 for no real gain. } 12. g4 Qe7 { Slightly worse for you: the hole on f6 is a real target for his pieces now. }) 12. g3 { He plays g3 to guard the pawn on h2. Now get your own king to safety. } 12... O-O { Castle! Your king reaches safety on g8, and now your rooks can swing to the open e column. [%csl Gg8] } (12... a5 { Pawn to a5 gains a little space on the wing before you castle. } 13. Rc1 O-O { Castle next move regardless: the pawn move cost nothing, since castling was always still waiting for you. }) (12... h5 { Pushing h5 goes for his king, but your own king is still stuck in the centre. } 13. Bg2 a5 { Slightly worse for you: chasing his king does little good while your own is still uncastled in the centre. }) (12... Ne4 { Knight to e4 jumps in, but he simply takes the pawn on e5, and the whole point of the isolani is gone. } 13. Bg2 a5 14. dxe5 { Slightly worse for you: the isolani pawn just traded itself away, and the bishop diagonal you paid for it went with it. }) (12... Qe7 { Queen to e7 moves off the back row, but it lets him push c5 with real bite. } 13. c5 Bc7 14. Bxb4 { Worse for you: the pawn on b4 falls for nothing, and retreating the bishop did not save it either. [%csl Rb4] }) 13. dxe5 { He captures on e5. Take back with the bishop, and it lands exactly where the isolani plan wanted it. } 13... Bxe5 { The bishop takes back and sits on e5, staring straight down the diagonal at b2 and h2. The isolani pawn was worth every bit of this. [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge5b2,Ge5h2] } (13... Nxe5 { Knight takes back on e5 instead, and the bishop stays home on f8 — the whole point of the isolani plan never arrives. } 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Bg2 { Fine, but slightly worse for you: the bishop's diagonal was the entire reason for taking the isolani, and this recapture skips it. }) (13... dxc4 { Taking on c4 leaves your own bishop on d6 hanging to the pawn. } 14. exd6 { A whole bishop falls, for nothing in return. [%csl Rd6] }) (13... Ne4 { Knight to e4 jumps in, but it does nothing to save the bishop on d6, which simply falls. } 14. exd6 { The bishop on d6 falls for nothing, and the knight jump does nothing to win it back. [%csl Rd6] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, the open column"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/U3Gx6y2G"]
[FEN "2r3k1/1b3pp1/p4n1p/1p1pq3/2nN4/P2RPP2/1P3BPP/1Q3BK1 b - - 6 29"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/1b3pp1/p4n1p/1p1pq3/2nN4/P2RPP2/1P3BPP/1Q3BK1_b_-_-_6_29"]

{ His isolated pawn on d5 — the isolani — is a real weakness, and he has no dark-squared bishop left to defend it. Where do your rooks want to go? [%csl Rd5] }
29... Nd7 { He steps his knight back to d7. The c column is wide open — take it before he does. } 30. Rc3 { Rook to c3 claims the only open column, and your queen is coming to c1 right behind it. [%cal Gc3c8,Gb1c1] } (30. Qa1 { Queen to a1 tucks into the corner, but it does nothing to fight for the open column. } 30... Nd6 31. Bg3 Qf6 { Slightly better for you, but the c column still is not yours. }) (30. Bg3 { Bishop to g3 hits his queen, and she simply steps aside. } 30... Qg5 31. Bf2 Qg6 { Slightly better for you: the queen retreats, but chasing her gains less than committing your rook to the c column right away. }) (30. h4 { Pawn to h4 edges forward on the wing, doing nothing for the fight over the c column. } 30... Nf8 31. Qa1 Ne6 { Slightly better for you: the rim pawn move never joined the fight for the c column at all. }) (30. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it spends a move on luft instead of grabbing the open column. } 30... Nf8 31. Rc3 Nd2 { Only a little better for you: the luft is safe enough, but the c column is still open for him to contest a move later. }) 30... Nf8 { He regroups his knight to f8. Now bring the queen up behind the rook. } 31. Qc1 { Queen to c1 completes the battery down the c column, and his knight on c4 is pinned in front of his own rook on c8. [%cal Gc1c8][%csl Rc4] } (31. Qc2 { Queen to c2 blocks your own rook's view down the column. } 31... Qe8 32. Qc1 Ne6 { Slightly better for you: the queen simply reroutes to c1 anyway, one move later than going there directly. }) (31. Bg3 { Bishop to g3 hits his queen again, and she simply steps away. } 31... Qe8 32. Qc1 a5 { Slightly better for you: chasing the queen costs him a tempo he did not really need to spend. }) (31. Qf5 { Queen to f5 wanders off toward the king side instead of joining the column fight. } 31... Qe8 32. Qb1 Nd2 { Only a little better for you: the queen finds an active square, but the tempo it costs lets him reposition his knight instead. }) (31. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow, and his knight simply jumps into d2. } 31... Nd2 32. Qc2 Nxf1 { Only a little better for you: the knight still jumps into d2, just one move later than it otherwise would have. }) 31... Ne6 { His knight lands on e6, hitting yours. Now push the pawn that finishes the job. } 32. b3 { Pawn to b3 kicks the knight off c4 for good, and the c column belongs to you alone now. [%cal Gb3c4][%csl Gc3] } (32. Bg3 { Bishop to g3 hits the queen again, but he simply trades knights on d4 and gives some of the tempo back. } 32... Qf6 33. b3 Nxd4 { Only a little better for you: hitting the queen wins a tempo, but the trade on d4 that follows hands some of it right back. }) (32. Bd3 { Bishop to d3 attacks the knight on c4, and he simply trades it off. } 32... Nxd4 33. exd4 Qe8 { Only a little better for you: the bishop wins a tempo, but the knight trade simplifies toward a position he is happier to defend. }) (32. Ne2 { Knight to e2 retreats to safety, giving up the fight for the c column for a move. } 32... Rc6 33. h3 Qf6 { Only a little better for you: the retreat is safe, but it hands him a free move to double his own pieces on the c column. }) (32. h4 { Pawn to h4 pushes on the wing, far from the c column where the real business is happening. } 32... Nxd4 33. exd4 Qe6 { Only a little better for you: the pawn push does not stop the trade on d4, and that trade suits him more than it suits you. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the minority attack, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/oAeT1bml"]
[FEN "3rrnk1/pp3ppp/1npqb3/3p4/1P1P4/2NBP2P/P1QN1PP1/1R3RK1 w - - 3 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3rrnk1/pp3ppp/1npqb3/3p4/1P1P4/2NBP2P/P1QN1PP1/1R3RK1_w_-_-_3_17"]

{ New plan: the minority attack — pushing your two queenside pawns against his three to leave one of his pawns permanently weak. Push b5, and his c pawn is weak for good. First, stop his c5 break. [%cal Yc3e2] }
17. Ne2 { Knight to e2 guards the pawn on d4, so his own break with c5 no longer wins anything. Now your own break, b5, is free to come. [%cal Gb4b5][%csl Gd4] } (17. Rfd1 { Rook to d1 also works: the rook develops first, and the knight to e2 still follows right after. } 17... a6 18. Ne2 g6 { The same minority attack plan arrives, just one move later than playing the knight first. }) (17. Rfc1 { Rook to c1 eyes the c column early, but his knight jumps into c4 before you can stop it. } 17... Nc4 18. Bxc4 dxc4 { Still clearly better for you, but you have to give up the bishop to remove the knight from c4. [%csl Rc4] }) (17. a4 { Pawn to a4 pushes ahead, but his knight still jumps into c4 first. } 17... Nc4 18. Nxc4 dxc4 { Still a bit better for you: the knight trade on c4 costs a little of your edge, but the minority attack plan is still intact. }) (17. b5 { Pushing b5 right away is too soon: his own break with c5 is ready to answer it. } 17... c5 18. dxc5 Qxc5 { Only a little better for you: his break on c5 frees his position, and the pressure you wanted to build never arrives. [%csl Gc5] }) 17... a6 { He plays a6 to stop b5 for now. Bring your own a pawn up instead: a4, then a5, and b5 follows. [%cal Ya2a4] } 18. a4 { Pawn to a4 loads the minority attack fully. b5 comes next, and however he recaptures, his c pawn ends up standing alone for good. [%cal Gb4b5][%tail] } (18. Rfe1 { Rook to e1 also works: the rook gets active first, and the minority attack still follows. } 18... g6 19. a4 Ra8 { The b5 break still comes right on schedule, just with your rook already active on e1. }) (18. a3 { Pawn to a3 only goes one step, which works but costs an extra tempo to finish the plan. } 18... Ra8 19. a4 Bd7 { You spend two moves getting the a pawn where a4 would have put it in one. }) (18. Rfd1 { Rook to d1 is fine too: the rook gets active, and a4 still follows right behind it. } 18... Ng6 19. a4 Ra8 { The very same minority attack arrives, just with the rook posted on d1 instead of staying home. }) (18. b5 { Pushing b5 without the a pawn's support loses the pawn outright. } 18... axb5 19. Nb3 Ra8 { Worse for you, exactly as advertised: the pawn on b5 is gone, and retreating the knight to b3 never wins it back. [%csl Rb5] }) 18... Rb8 19. b5 19... axb5 20. axb5 20... Rec8 21. bxc6 21... bxc6 22. Rfc1 { The rook joins in on c1, right behind the queen. Two pieces are already aimed at the lone pawn on c6, with no black pawn left anywhere near to defend it. [%csl Rc6] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the minority attack, or the centre"]
