[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b3k1/p1r2ppp/1ppq1n2/3p4/Q2P1N2/P3P1P1/1P3PBP/2R1R1K1_w_-_-_6_19"]

{ The minority attack is not your only plan here. Your bishop sits on the long diagonal from g2 — which pawn break suits it best? }
19. f3 { Pawn to f3 prepares e4 next. The bishop on g2 wakes up along its diagonal, and the centre opens on your own terms. [%cal Ge3e4][%csl Gg2] } (19. Qb4 { Queen to b4 dances around, but it changes nothing about the position. } 19... Qd8 20. Qd2 Ne4 { Only a little better for you: the queen trip gains nothing, and his knight jumps into e4 while you were busy dancing. }) (19. h4 { Pawn to h4 is a rim move that does not touch the centre at all. } 19... h6 20. Qb4 Qd8 { Only a little better for you: a harmless edge pawn push, but it is not the break you came here to play. }) (19. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: a rim move while f3 and e4 were both ready to go right now. } 19... g5 20. Ne2 Bf5 { Only a little better for you: the rim pawn does not open the centre, and he gets on with his own plan instead. }) (19. b4 { Pawn to b4 tries the minority attack instead, but his bishop comes out and your own queenside pawns freeze up. } 19... Bf5 20. Qb3 Rac8 { Equal: your queenside pawns lock solid, and his bishop claims a diagonal instead of yours. [%csl Rb4] }) (19. Nxd5 { Knight takes the pawn on d5, but the c pawn simply recaptures, and the knight is gone for good. } 19... cxd5 { You gave up a whole knight and only got a single pawn for it. [%csl Rd5] }) 19... g5 { He kicks the knight with g5. Where does it retreat to? } 20. Nd3 { Knight to d3 guards the square e5 and stays close to the centre. Now push e4. [%cal Ge3e4] } (20. Ne2 { Knight to e2 also works: it heads back toward c3 instead. } 20... Bf5 21. Nc3 g4 { Still clearly better for you: the knight finds its way to c3, but the e4 break is one move further away. }) (20. Nh3 { Knight to h3 retreats to the rim, and he simply trades it off. } 20... Bxh3 21. Bxh3 Re8 { Equal: your bishop ends up on h3, far from the e4 square it wanted to support. }) (20. e4 { Pushing e4 right away plays the break, but it leaves the knight on f4 hanging. } 20... gxf4 21. e5 Qd8 { Worse for you: a whole knight is gone for nothing. [%csl Rf4] }) (20. Nxd5 { Knight takes the pawn on d5 again, but this time the rooks are about to be traded off too. } 20... cxd5 21. Rxc7 Qxc7 { Lost: the rooks come off the board, and you are left a whole knight down for nothing. }) 20... Be6 { His bishop develops to e6. Now play the break you have been preparing. } 21. e4 { Pawn to e4! The centre opens at last, and the bishop on g2 suddenly sees the whole board. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gg2d5] } (21. Qb4 { Queen to b4 retreats to safety, and he uses the free move to untangle his pieces. } 21... Ne8 22. Qd2 h6 { Only a little better for you: the retreat is safe enough, but he spends the free move untangling his own pieces. }) (21. Nf2 { Knight to f2 is slow: the retreat does not open the centre any faster than playing e4 outright. } 21... Rd8 22. Qc2 Qe7 { Only a little better for you: the retreat is tidy, but the centre stays shut a move longer than it needed to. }) (21. Qb3 { Queen to b3 steps back to a safer square. } 21... Rac8 22. Qc3 Nd7 { Only a little better for you: the queen finds a safe square, but his rook reaches c8 in the meantime. }) (21. Qc2 { Queen to c2 is slow too: the move does nothing for the e4 break either. } 21... Rac8 22. Qc3 Nd7 { Only a little better for you: the queen shuffles about, and his rook claims the c column while you were busy shuffling. }) 1-0


