[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4bk1/rbpn1p2/1p2qnpp/1P1p4/3P4/2NBP2P/1BQN1PP1/1R2R1K1_w_-_-_8_21"]

{ The minority attack has already done its work: his pawns sit on a7, b6 and c7. There is another break waiting in the centre. Which pawn plays it? [%csl Gd5] }
21. e4 { Pawn to e4! The centre blows wide open, and your bishop on d3 and rook on e1 both point straight at his king. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd3h7] } (21. Rbc1 { Rook to c1 develops, but it leaves the centre closed and the e4 break still waiting. } 21... Bg7 22. Ne2 Ne8 { Still clearly better for you, but slow: the centre stays shut a move longer than it needed to. }) (21. f4 { Pushing f4 opens a different central lever, but it hands his knight the e4 square instead of you. } 21... Qe7 22. Nf3 Ne4 { Still clearly better for you, but the key square e4 belongs to his knight instead of your pieces. [%csl Re4] }) (21. Rbd1 { Rook to d1 is slow: a developing move while e4 was ready to be played right now. } 21... Bg7 22. Qb3 Kh7 { Still clearly better for you: the position stays closed, and your bishop on b2 never wakes up along its diagonal. }) (21. Nf3 { Knight to f3 develops, but his own knight simply jumps into e4 first. } 21... Ne4 22. Bxe4 dxe4 { Still clearly better for you, but the e4 square now belongs to him instead of you. [%csl Re4] }) 21... Nh5 { His knight jumps to the rim on h5, a poor square. Take the pawn on d5 now. } 22. Nxd5 { Knight takes on d5! His bishop must recapture, and then your pawn retakes, landing a monster on d5. [%cal Gb7d5,Ge4d5] } (22. Be2 { Bishop to e2 also works: it eyes the knight on h5 first, before taking on d5. } 22... Ng7 23. exd5 Qf6 { Still clearly winning: the knight retreats to safety, and the centre still opens exactly the same way. }) (22. exd5 { Pawn takes on d5 directly: also good, the very same idea in a different move order. } 22... Qf6 23. Nf3 Nf4 { Still clearly winning: the same monster pawn lands on d5, just reached in the other move order. }) (22. Nf3 { Knight to f3 is slow: it lets him meet the eventual d5 push with his knight already posted on f4. } 22... Nf4 23. exd5 Qd6 { Still clearly better for you: the pawn still lands on d5, just with his knight already settled on f4. }) (22. e5 { Pushing e5 closes the centre again instead of taking on d5. } 22... Nf4 23. Nf1 c6 { Still better for you, but less: his knight gets to settle on f4 while the centre stays shut. [%csl Rf4] }) 22... Bxd5 { He recaptures with the bishop. Now retake with the pawn. } 23. exd5 { A pawn sits on the monster square d5, his queen is hit, and the bishop on d3 stares straight at his king. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd3h7] } 1-0


