[FEN "2r3k1/3q1pbp/1pn3p1/p2pPp2/3P1P1B/PQP2N2/6PP/5RK1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/3q1pbp/1pn3p1/p2pPp2/3P1P1B/PQP2N2/6PP/5RK1_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ Both sides carry a backward pawn from the minority attack you played earlier: yours on c3, his on b6. He wants to run his pawn to b5. Stop that, for ever. [%csl Rb6,Rc3] }
1. a4 { Pawn to a4 guards the b5 square for good, so his backward pawn on b6 can never advance again. [%csl Rb6,Gb5][%tail] } (1. Rb1 { Rook to b1 develops first, but he simply runs his pawn through to a4. } 1... a4 2. Qxb6 g5 { Only a little better for you: a messy fight follows, with his pawn now sitting on a4 instead of contained. [%csl Ra4] }) (1. Qxb6 { Queen grabs the pawn on b6 right away, but it wakes his rook up onto the open b column. } 1... Rb8 2. Qa6 Bh6 { Only a little better for you: a clean pawn up, but his pieces come alive to fight for it back. }) (1. Re1 { Rook to e1 develops, but it lets him push his pawn through to b5. } 1... b5 2. e6 fxe6 { Equal: he gets his own break in, and the backward pawn on b6 is never fixed at all. [%csl Gb5] }) (1. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow, and it lets him push through to b5 all the same. } 1... b5 2. Qxb5 Nxe5 { Equal: your queen grabs a pawn on b5, but his knight grabs one right back to even the score. }) 1... Nd8 2. Qxb6 { The queen simply takes the pawn on b6. A clean pawn up, and his whole queenside is falling apart. [%csl Gb6] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: give a pawn, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/NjC9EHjz"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/ppppbp1p/6p1/3P4/8/4Q1P1/PP2PP1P/R1B1KBNR w KQkq - 1 9"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqk2r/ppppbp1p/6p1/3P4/8/4Q1P1/PP2PP1P/R1B1KBNR_w_KQkq_-_1_9"]

{ New plan: give a pawn, get the game — sometimes giving up a pawn buys you something worth more, like a wrecked pawn structure or a lead in development. One pawn push wrecks his for ever. [%cal Yd5d6] }
9. d6 { Pawn to d6! He is forced to take, and his pawns end up doubled on the d column for good — a pawn well spent. [%csl Gd6] } (9. Qh6 { Queen to h6 is also strong: it stops him castling king side. But it leaves his pawn structure untouched. } 9... d6 10. Nf3 Nd7 { Still clearly better for you, but it settles into a normal game instead of the wrecked structure the pawn push gives. }) (9. Nf3 { Knight to f3 also works: natural development, though it adds no extra pressure to the doubled pawns yet. } 9... O-O 10. Qh6 Bb4+ { Fine for you, but his pawns are never actually wrecked this way. }) (9. Bd2 { Bishop to d2 develops, but it lets him castle into safety. } 9... O-O 10. Bc3 Bf6 { Only a little better for you: a natural developing move, but it gives him a free move to get his king to safety. }) (9. h4 { Pawn to h4 creeps forward on the rim, far from the real business in the centre. } 9... O-O 10. Nf3 Re8 { Only a little better for you: the edge pawn does nothing to stop him castling into safety. }) (9. Qxe7+ { Taking the bishop on e7 walks the queen right into a recapture — his queen simply takes yours back, and you have given away a queen for a bishop. } 9... Qxe7 10. Nf3 Na6 { Lost: down a queen for a bishop, the pawn on d5 falling next barely matters. [%csl Rd5] }) 9... cxd6 { He is forced to take on d6. Now bring the knight around: to h3, then f4, then d5. [%cal Yg1h3,Gh3f4] } 10. Nh3 { Knight to h3, on its way to f4 and then d5. His pawns on d6 and d7 are doubled and weak for good. [%cal Gh3f4,Gf4d5][%csl Rd6][%tail] } (10. Bg2 { Bishop to g2 develops first. Fine, but the knight's road to f4 is still open whenever you want it. } 10... Nc6 11. Bd2 O-O { Still clearly better for you: the bishop develops, and the knight still has its whole road to f4 waiting. }) (10. Bd2 { Bishop to d2 is slow: development that does nothing to chase the doubled pawns. } 10... O-O 11. Bg2 Nc6 { Still clearly better for you: he gets to castle first, but the knight is still headed for f4 regardless. }) (10. h4 { Pushing h4 storms the king side, but without the pieces behind it, it accomplishes little. } 10... Nc6 11. Nf3 d5 { Only a little better for you: the pawns advance, but without pieces behind them, he equalises in the centre. }) (10. Qd3 { Queen to d3 adds pressure to d6, but he simply develops and the pressure comes to nothing yet. } 10... Nc6 11. Bg2 O-O { Only a little better for you: the queen finds a good square, but the pressure on d6 comes to nothing yet while he develops. }) 10... O-O 11. Nf4 { Knight to f4: halfway home to d5, and his pawns on d6 and d7 are still doubled and going nowhere. [%csl Rd6,Rd7] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: give a pawn, castle anyway"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/9tZtwCFQ"]
[FEN "3qk2r/p2pb1pp/1p2p3/4PpB1/3Pb2P/5N2/PP2QPP1/2r2RK1 w k - 0 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3qk2r/p2pb1pp/1p2p3/4PpB1/3Pb2P/5N2/PP2QPP1/2r2RK1_w_k_-_0_17"]

{ You are under real pressure: his rook wants c7 and his queen wants h5. Giving a pawn to castle is the price on the table — is it worth paying? }
17. Rxc1 { He recaptures on c1. Your queen cannot defend everything at once — what do you do now? [%csl Rc7] } 17... O-O { Castle! Yes, a pawn goes, but your king reaches safety and your pieces are finally free to work. [%csl Gg8] } (17... Bxf3 { Trading on f3 first removes a defender, but it hands his queen the active square f3 for free. } 18. Qxf3 Bxg5 19. hxg5 O-O { Also fine for you, but the trade only helps his queen find an active post. }) (17... Bc6 { Bishop to c6 guards the pawn, but your king stays stuck in the middle of the board. } 18. Bxe7 Kxe7 19. Qa6 { Slightly worse for you: the pawn on a7 falls anyway, and your king is still stuck in the centre. [%csl Re7] }) (17... h6 { Pawn to h6 kicks the bishop away, but your king still stays home in the centre. } 18. Bxe7 Kxe7 19. Nd2 { Slightly worse for you: keeping your king in the centre costs more than the bishop pair is worth. }) (17... Qa8 { Queen to a8 tucks into the corner just to guard pawns — a passive square for her. } 18. Nd2 Bd5 19. Nc4 { Worse for you: your queen is stuck babysitting pawns instead of doing anything active. [%csl Ra8] }) 18. Bxe7 { He captures your bishop on e7. Recapture with the queen. } 18... Qxe7 { The queen recaptures. You gave up a pawn, but your king is safe and your other bishop is active on e4 — a fair price. [%csl Gg8][%tail] } 19. Rc7 19... h6 { You give your king a little air with h6. Nothing is hanging, and your bishop on e4 is worth every bit as much as his rook on the seventh row. [%csl Ge4] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: give a pawn, keep what you have"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/uI1zznqH"]
[FEN "r1bn2k1/1p3p2/p3pp1p/2p5/4P3/8/PPN1BPPP/4K2R w K - 0 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bn2k1/1p3p2/p3pp1p/2p5/4P3/8/PPN1BPPP/4K2R_w_K_-_0_17"]

{ You are a pawn up from the give a pawn plan a few moves back, and the temptation is to fix your doubled pawns right away. Do not — make something active of what you already have. }
17. O-O { He castles into safety. Your pawns are doubled and your own king already sits on g8 — what is the plan from here? } 17... Nc6 { Knight to c6 develops naturally, keeping the structure compact. The king walks up to join in next. [%csl Gc6][%cal Gg8f8,Gf8e7] } (17... Kf8 { King to f8 also works: the king starts walking first, and the knight follows right after. } 18. Rd1 Nc6 { The knight still lands on c6 either way, just after the king moves instead of before it. }) (17... Bd7 { Bishop to d7 also works: it develops the bishop immediately instead of the knight. } 18. Rd1 Bc6 { Solid for you: compact and safe, though the bishop ends up a little passive on c6. }) (17... b5 { Pawn to b5 grabs space, but it lets his rook come into the open column. } 18. b3 Nc6 19. Rc1 { Still better for you, but less: the rook's entry costs you a slice of your edge. }) (17... e5 { Pushing e5 fixes your doubled pawn, but it hands him the d5 square and a whole diagonal for his pieces. } 18. Ne3 Be6 19. Bc4 { Only a little better for you: his pieces come alive on the squares you just gave away. [%csl Rd5] }) 18. Rd1 { His rook claims the open column. Now start walking your king up. } 18... Kf8 { The king steps up to f8. In an endgame the king is a fighting piece: it is heading for e7 and then d6. [%cal Gf8e7] } (18... Rb8 { Rook to b8 moves first, and the king's walk waits a move. } 19. Rd3 Kf8 { Still clearly better for you: the king still gets to walk up, just a move later than starting right away. }) (18... b5 { Pawn to b5 grabs more space, but it lets his rook land on d6. } 19. Rd6 Bb7 { Still better for you, but his rook reaching d6 costs you a slice of your edge. [%csl Rd6] }) (18... e5 { Pushing e5 gives up the d5 square all over again. } 19. Rd5 Be6 20. Rxc5 { Only a little better for you: a pawn is gone, eating into the material edge you started with. }) (18... Kg7 { King to g7 heads the wrong way — in the endgame, your king belongs in the centre. } 19. f4 Kf8 { Still clearly better for you, but you have to turn the king around and walk the right way after all. }) 19. Kf1 { His own king comes out to meet yours. Keep walking toward the centre. } 19... Ke7 { The king comes to e7, heading for d6. In an endgame, an extra pawn is an extra pawn, ugly structure or not. [%csl Ge7][%tail] } (19... b6 { Pawn to b6 also works: it opens a diagonal for the bishop to reach b7. } 20. Rd3 Bb7 { Still clearly better for you: the bishop gets a nice diagonal, and the king marches on just the same. }) (19... b5 { Pawn to b5 is fine, though it leaves the pawn itself a little loose. } 20. Rd3 Ne5 { Still clearly better for you: the knight finds a strong post on e5, though the pawn on b5 is a little exposed. }) (19... Rb8 { Rook to b8 is slow: a rook move while the king still has not even started walking. } 20. Rd3 b5 { Still clearly better for you: even without the king's help yet, the extra pawn still tells in the long run. }) (19... Kg7 { King to g7 heads the wrong way again, away from the centre where it is needed. } 20. Rd3 b5 { Still clearly better for you, but the king is not helping the cause from over there. }) 20. Rd3 20... Bd7 21. Rh3 21... Nd4 22. Nxd4 22... cxd4 { You recapture toward the centre, and the pawn on d4 is passed — no white pawn left on the board can ever stop it, and you are still a full pawn up besides. [%csl Gd4] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: give a pawn, fix his pawns"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/FrDFQwGc"]
[FEN "r1bq1r2/pp2p1bk/3p2p1/P2Pnp1p/4P2P/R1N1BP2/1P1QBP2/4K2R b K - 3 16"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1r2/pp2p1bk/3p2p1/P2Pnp1p/4P2P/R1N1BP2/1P1QBP2/4K2R_b_K_-_3_16"]

{ He wants to play f4 himself and free his cramped position. Give a pawn of your own to deny him that — one pawn move settles the whole question. }
16... f4 { Pawn to f4! His bishop takes it, but his pawns are now stuck on light squares for good, and the f4 square is never his to use. [%csl Rf3,Re4][%cal Gf8f7] } (16... Rf7 { Rook to f7 is a useful move, but it lets him play his own f4 break first. } 17. f4 Ng4 18. Bd4 { Equal: his bishop settles happily on d4, and the freeing break you wanted to deny him lands anyway. [%csl Gf4] }) (16... Bd7 { Bishop to d7 develops, but it also lets his f4 break come through. } 17. f4 Ng4 18. Bd4 { Equal: his bishop centralises on d4, and he already gets the very break you meant to deny him. }) (16... Nf7 { Knight to f7 retreats, but his own knight jumps into the strong b5 square. } 17. Nb5 fxe4 18. fxe4 { Slightly worse for you: his knight lands on the strong b5 square while yours only went backward. }) (16... Bf6 { Bishop to f6 moves off its post, and he simply claims the diagonal it left behind. } 17. Bd4 f4 18. Nb5 { Slightly worse for you: leaving the long diagonal lets his bishop claim d4, and his knight already eyes the pawn on a7 from b5. }) (16... Nxf3+ { Knight takes the pawn on f3 with check, but the bishop simply recaptures, and the knight is lost for nothing. } 17. Bxf3 { A whole knight given away for a single pawn. [%csl Rf3] }) 17. Bxf4 { He recaptures on f4. Now the f column is yours to claim. } 17... Rf7 { Rook to f7: the f column belongs to you, and the bishop on f4 stands as a clear target straight down it. [%cal Gf7f4,Gd8f8] } (17... Bd7 { Bishop to d7 is also playable: the same exchange sacrifice on f4 still comes, just a move later. } 18. Nd1 Rxf4 19. Qxf4 Bh6 { Equal: the exchange sacrifice on f4 still works, just with the bishop already developed to d7. }) (17... b6 { Pawn to b6 grabs space, but it gives him time to castle into safety. } 18. axb6 Qxb6 19. O-O { Slightly worse for you: his king castles to safety, and the pawn trade only opens the a column for his rook. }) (17... Rf6 { Rook to f6 blocks its own bishop's diagonal, weakening the exchange sacrifice that follows. } 18. Nd1 Rxf4 19. Qxf4 Bh6 { Slightly worse for you: the rook on f6 blocks its own bishop's diagonal, so the same sacrifice achieves less. }) (17... Rxf4 { An exchange sacrifice: your rook for his bishop, chasing activity and the bishop pair in return. } 18. Qxf4 Bh6 19. Qg3 Qf8 { Slightly worse for you: he keeps the balance, and you are left the exchange down for your trouble. }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: space, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/LQLpaQfD"]
[FEN "r1b1r1k1/1pp1n1pp/p1pb1p2/8/4PP2/1NN5/PPP3PP/R1B2RK1 w - - 0 12"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b1r1k1/1pp1n1pp/p1pb1p2/8/4PP2/1NN5/PPP3PP/R1B2RK1_w_-_-_0_12"]

{ New plan: space — a pawn push that cramps his pieces behind their own pawns, robbing them of squares to stand on. Trade his good bishop, then invade. Push the f pawn. [%cal Yf4f5][%csl Rc8] }
12. f5 { Pawn to f5! His bishop on c8 is buried with nowhere safe to go but d7, the square e6 is yours, and f4 is free for a bishop trade. [%csl Rc8,Ge6][%cal Gc1f4] } (12. a3 { Pawn to a3 is quiet, but it gives him time to get ready for the f5 push. } 12... b6 13. f5 Bb7 { Slightly worse for you: his bishop gets out to b7 first, before the space plan can shut it in. [%csl Gb7] }) (12. Kf2 { King to f2 tidies up first, but it does nothing for the space plan. } 12... b6 13. Re1 Bb7 { Slightly worse for you: without f5 to shut it in, his bishop gets out to b7 with time to spare. }) (12. Bd2 { Bishop to d2 develops, but it lets his a pawn run forward unchallenged. } 12... a5 13. Rae1 a4 { Slightly worse for you: his queenside pawns roll forward while the space plan waits. }) (12. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it does nothing for your space plan while he gets on with his own. } 12... a5 13. a3 Bd7 { Slightly worse for you: his bishop reaches d7 in peace, since your h pawn move never challenged anything. }) 12... b6 { He wants his bishop out on b7. Trade his good bishop before it gets the chance. [%cal Yc1f4] } 13. Bf4 { Bishop to f4 challenges his dark-squared bishop, the better of his two, and offers the trade. [%cal Gf4d6] } (13. a3 { Pawn to a3 is slow, and it leaves him his whole bishop pair intact. } 13... Bb7 14. Bf4 Bxf4 { The trade on f4 still happens all the same, just a move later than playing it right away. }) (13. g4 { Pawn to g4 storms forward, but it skips the bishop trade the plan actually needs. } 13... Bb7 14. Bf4 Bxf4 { The bishop trade still comes right after, just following the pawn storm instead of leading it. }) (13. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it stalls the bishop trade you already set up with f5, and gives him a free tempo. } 13... Bb7 14. Bf4 Bxf4 { The bishop trade still happens on f4, but the h3 move lets him develop his other bishop for free in the meantime. }) (13. a4 { Pawn to a4 gains a little space on the edge, but does nothing for the bishop trade. } 13... Bb7 14. Bf4 Bxf4 { The trade on f4 goes the same way regardless, and the pawn on a4 did nothing to help you get there. }) 13... Bxf4 { He captures on f4. Take back with the rook. [%cal Yf1f4] } 14. Rxf4 { The rook takes back and sits actively on f4. Now bring the knight: to a4, hitting both b6 and c5. [%csl Gf4][%cal Gc3a4] } 14... c5 { He pushes his pawn to c5. Now jump the knight to a4 and squeeze both weaknesses. [%cal Yc3a4] } 15. Na4 { Knight to a4: the pawns on b6 and c5 are both targets, and d6 is a hole in his camp. Constrain first, then invade. [%cal Ga4b6,Ga4c5][%csl Gd6] } (15. Rd1 { Rook to d1 also works: it claims the open column instead. } 15... Bb7 16. Rf2 Rab8 { Still clearly better for you: the open column is useful, but nothing is constraining his b6 and c5 pawns yet. }) (15. Nd2 { Knight to d2 heads for c4 by the longer road. Fine, but slower. } 15... Rb8 16. Nc4 Rd8 { Still clearly better for you: the knight gets there by the longer road, and he gets a free move while it travels. }) (15. Rf2 { Rook to f2 tidies up, but it leaves his queenside pawns completely untouched. } 15... Rb8 16. Nd2 Bb7 { Still clearly better for you: a natural move, but it leaves his queenside pawns completely untouched. }) (15. a4 { Pawn to a4 claims the same square the knight wanted, but a pawn cannot hop into b6 or c5 the way a knight can. } 15... Rb8 16. a5 c4 { Still clearly better for you: the pawn claims the same square, but unlike the knight it can never hop into b6 or c5. }) (15. Nd4 { Knight to d4 jumps in, but the pawn on c5 simply takes it. } 15... cxd4 { A whole knight lost for nothing. [%csl Rd4] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: space, the breakthrough"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/C2dTNu1p"]
[FEN "2kr3r/pp3pp1/2p1pnp1/2n5/2P3P1/P2PP3/1BK1BP1P/R6R b - - 1 16"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2kr3r/pp3pp1/2p1pnp1/2n5/2P3P1/P2PP3/1BK1BP1P/R6R_b_-_-_1_16"]

{ The space plan already has his king side pawns stuck in place, and your bishop on b2 wants the long diagonal thrown open. Which pawn is the one to break through? }
16... g5 { He locks the king side shut with g5. The h column is his unless you break it open by force. [%csl Rg5] } 17. h4 { Pawn to h4! He is forced to take, and pushing g5 next shuts his knight out for good. The bishop on b2 then owns the whole long diagonal. [%cal Gh4g5,Gb2h8] } (17. h3 { Pawn to h3 only goes half the distance, and nothing about the position actually changes. } 17... Rh7 18. Rag1 Rdh8 { Only a little better for you: half a step towards the break, and he simply doubles his rooks on the h column to meet it. }) (17. Bd4 { Bishop to d4 hits the knight on c5, and he simply steps it aside. } 17... Ncd7 18. h4 Rxh4 { Only a little better for you: the bishop wins a tempo, but he steps aside, and the pawn break now costs you a pawn to force through. }) (17. Be5 { Bishop to e5 jumps into the centre, but it gives him time to swing his rook to h3. } 17... Rh3 18. Bg3 Re8 { Only a little better for you: the bishop centralises well, but his rook reaches h3 and starts making noise of its own. }) (17. Rab1 { Rook to b1 doubles on a column that has nothing on it to attack. } 17... Rh3 18. Be5 Nfd7 { Equal: the rook heads down an empty column, and his own rook claims h3 completely for free. [%csl Rb1] }) (17. Bxf6 { Trading on f6 gives up your best bishop, the very one the break was meant to unleash. } 17... gxf6 18. d4 Nd7 { Worse for you: his king side is safe and sound now, and the bishop that wanted the long diagonal is gone. [%csl Rb2] }) 17... gxh4 { He is forced to take on h4. Now shut the door on his knight. } 18. g5 { Pawn to g5 kicks the knight away, and his own h pawn is cut off behind it for good. Rooks to the h column next. [%csl Gg5][%cal Gh1h4] } (18. Rag1 { Rook to g1 also works, doubling first before the pawn push. } 18... Rdg8 19. g5 Nfd7 { The g pawn still breaks through just as well, just a move later — and he spends his own free tempo doubling rooks on the open column. }) (18. Bd4 { Bishop to d4 chases the knight back first, before playing the pawn break. } 18... Ncd7 19. g5 Ne8 { Fine for you, just slower: the bishop chases the knight back first, and the pawn break still comes one move behind schedule. }) (18. f4 { Pawn to f4 is a useful central move, but it is not the urgent one here. } 18... Rdg8 19. Rh2 Nfd7 { Only a little better for you: a natural pawn move, but he doubles his rooks on the g column while you play it. }) (18. Rh2 { Rook to h2 lines up on the h column first, before playing the pawn break. } 18... Rh7 19. Rah1 Rdh8 { Only a little better for you: the rook lines up first, but he matches you column for column before the pawn ever moves. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: space, invade on the open column"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/uDudRkx6"]
[FEN "1n1q1rk1/Nb2ppbp/pp4p1/3p4/3Pn3/BP1BPN2/P3QPPP/2R3K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1n1q1rk1/Nb2ppbp/pp4p1/3p4/3Pn3/BP1BPN2/P3QPPP/2R3K1_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ The space plan has paid off: the c column is open and yours, but a rook alone is not enough to break through. Bring the queen up behind it, and invade. }
1. Qc2 { Queen to c2 completes the battery down the c column, with the square c7 as the target. [%cal Gc2c7][%csl Gc7] } (1. h4 { Pawn to h4 first also works: the queen still reaches c2, just one move slower. } 1... b5 2. Qc2 Qb6 { Still clearly winning for you: the queen still lands on c2, just one move later than going there directly. }) (1. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: a useful little move perhaps, but it does nothing to get your queen behind the rook. } 1... b5 2. Qc2 Qb6 { Still clearly winning for you: the queen reaches c2 all the same, one tempo behind schedule. }) (1. Qd1 { Queen to d1 heads the wrong way entirely, away from the column it wants. } 1... b5 2. Qc2 Qb6 { Still clearly better for you: the queen simply comes to c2 anyway, one move later. }) (1. Bb4 { Bishop to b4 develops, but he simply kicks it away with f6. } 1... f6 2. Qc2 a5 { Still clearly better for you, but slower: the bishop trip costs a tempo the direct plan does not need. }) (1. Bxe7 { Taking the pawn on e7 looks free at first glance. } 1... Qxe7 { It is not: his queen simply recaptures, and you have given up a whole bishop for a single pawn. [%csl Re7] }) 1... a5 { He pushes his pawn to a5. Now jump the knight into the centre. } 2. Ne5 { Knight to e5 blocks his bishop's diagonal and attacks the knight on e4 at the same time. [%csl Ge5] } (2. Qc7 { Queen to c7 also works: straight into the invasion right away. } 2... Qxc7 3. Rxc7 Ba6 { Winning: your rook lands on the seventh row, and the invasion is already complete. }) (2. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it does not challenge the knight on e4 or block the bishop the way the knight jump does. } 2... Bf6 3. Qc7 Ba6 { Still clearly winning for you: the invasion on c7 arrives all the same, just without first dealing with his pieces in the centre. }) (2. h4 { Pawn to h4 is slow: a king side pawn move that leaves the centre exactly as it was. } 2... Bf6 3. h5 Na6 { Still clearly winning for you: the pawn storm rolls on, but his pieces get a free move to reorganise. }) (2. g3 { Pawn to g3 is slow: it shores up the king a little, but does nothing about the knight on e4. } 2... Bf6 3. Qc7 Ba6 { Still clearly winning for you: c7 still falls to the invasion, just a move later than it needed to. }) 2... Bxe5 { He trades on e5. Recapture with the pawn. } 3. dxe5 { The pawn recaptures, and it shuts his own knight in behind it. } (3. Qc7 { Queen to c7 jumps in a move too soon, and his bishop simply eats it. } 3... Bxc7 { A whole queen lost for nothing. [%csl Rc7] }) (3. Bxe4 { Taking the knight on e4 wins a piece, but his bishop simply escapes through the trades that follow. } 3... Bd6 4. Bxd6 Qxd6 { Only a little better for you: everything gets traded off, and the invasion loses most of its bite. }) 3... Ng5 { The knight retreats to g5. Now finish the invasion. } 4. Qc7 { Queen to c7! He is forced to trade, and your rook lands on the seventh row behind it. [%cal Gc7d8,Gc1c7] } (4. f4 { Pushing f4 kicks the knight first. Also good, just slower. } 4... Ne6 5. f5 Nc5 { Still clearly winning for you: the knight gets kicked back just the same, and the attack rolls on a move slower. }) (4. Bb5 { Bishop to b5 develops, but it hits nothing useful from there. } 4... Ne6 5. f4 Kh8 { Still clearly winning for you, just slower: the bishop trip does not add anything to the attack. }) (4. Be2 { Bishop to e2 is slow: a purely defensive move that does nothing to kick the knight off g5. } 4... Ne6 5. Bb5 d4 { Still clearly better for you: the bishop gets to b5 eventually, just a tempo later than going there directly. }) (4. h4 { Pawn to h4 also kicks the knight away. } 4... Ne6 5. Bb5 d4 { Still clearly better for you: the knight gets kicked, but the bishop and the central break still take an extra move to arrive. }) 1-0
